    introHeading: "What is Residential Rehabilitation?",
    intro: ["Residential rehabilitation is a form of brain injury care in which a person lives on site while taking part in a structured rehabilitation program. Instead of traveling to appointments, therapy, supervision and everyday support are organized around the place where the person lives, so practice can continue through the whole day.", "Irvine has offered residential care since it began in 1986 as Irvine AFC, an adult foster care facility with a Closed Head Injury program. In 1992 it became Irvine Head Injury Home, providing cognitive rehabilitation and residential care for persons with closed head injuries. Today, assisted living apartments, sub-acute rooms and adult foster care homes at the Southfield campus are at the heart of our in-patient care, and most of the people we serve are treated on an in-patient basis.", "Residential programs are typically considered when a brain injury affects safety, memory, behavior or the ability to manage daily life without support. The right level of care depends on the person, and our multi-disciplinary team works with each resident and family to decide what fits."],
