    extraSections: [{"heading": "Residential settings on our campus", "body": ["The Southfield campus at 25700 Lahser Road includes assisted living apartments, sub-acute rooms and adult foster care homes. These settings allow the team to match the level of support to each person, from close supervision to greater independence.", "In 2003 Irvine added a special needs unit for individuals requiring nursing with physician supervision. For people who are ready for more independence, the semi-independent apartment program allows residents to receive therapy in their own apartment.", "Irvine Neuro Rehabilitation is licensed by the State of Michigan and accredited by CARF, the Commission on Accreditation of Rehabilitation Facilities."]}],
    faqs: [{"q": "Who is residential rehabilitation for?", "a": "It is for people whose brain injury makes it hard to live safely or independently without support, or who need several therapies at once. Irvine serves adults and children, men and women, with mild and severe injuries. A consultation helps decide whether residential care is the right fit."}, {"q": "What does the living environment look like?", "a": "Residential care takes place in assisted living apartments, sub-acute rooms and adult foster care homes on our Southfield campus. The environment is designed to be homelike."}, {"q": "Can our family visit the campus first?", "a": "Yes. You can schedule a tour by calling (248) 415-2500, and there is no charge for a consultation."}, {"q": "How long do people stay?", "a": "Length of stay depends on the person's needs, goals and progress, and on what coverage allows. The team reviews the plan regularly and discusses next steps with the resident and family."}, {"q": "How are referrals made?", "a": "Referrals can be faxed to (248) 415-2510. You can also call (248) 415-2500 to talk with the team about next steps."}],
