    extraSections: [{"heading":"Treatments and techniques","body":["Depending on your needs, physical therapy may include therapeutic exercise for strength and flexibility, balance and gait training, neuromuscular re-education, manual techniques, stretching, functional mobility practice such as transfers and stair climbing, and education on posture, pacing, and injury prevention.","Because rehabilitation often involves more than one need, our team includes professionals in occupational therapy, speech therapy, psychiatry, and counseling and case management. This allows care to be coordinated when recovery touches daily activities, communication, mood, or practical support."]}],
    faqs: [{"q":"What conditions can physical therapy help with?","a":"Physical therapy is used for a wide range of problems, including movement difficulties after stroke or brain injury, balance problems, orthopedic injuries, post-surgical recovery, and chronic pain. Your evaluation determines whether it is a good fit for you."},{"q":"Do I need a referral?","a":"Referral requirements depend on your situation and your insurance. The simplest step is to call us at (248) 415-2500 and we can talk through what to do next."},{"q":"What should I wear and bring to my first visit?","a":"Wear comfortable clothing and supportive shoes that allow you to move freely. Bring a list of your medications, any relevant medical records, and a list of your questions and goals."},{"q":"Will physical therapy be painful?","a":"Therapy should challenge you, but it should not cause severe pain. Some soreness after new exercises is common. Tell your therapist how you feel so the plan can be adjusted."},{"q":"How long will I need treatment?","a":"Length of care depends on your condition, your goals, and how your body responds. Some people need a few weeks, while others benefit from longer-term support. Your plan is reviewed regularly."}],
