    faqs: [{"q":"What does an occupational therapist do?","a":"An occupational therapist helps people perform the everyday activities that make up daily life, including self-care, household tasks, work and leisure. Therapy may involve building skills, changing how a task is done, adapting tools, or modifying the environment."},{"q":"Who can benefit from occupational therapy?","a":"People recovering from brain injury, stroke, or other neurological conditions often benefit, as do those with cognitive, motor or sensory difficulties that interfere with daily routines. If everyday tasks have become harder, an evaluation is a good place to start."},{"q":"Does occupational therapy address thinking and memory problems?","a":"Yes. Cognitive rehabilitation is a common part of occupational therapy. It can include strategies for attention, memory, planning and organization, practiced through tasks that resemble your real routines."},{"q":"How long will I need therapy?","a":"The length of care depends on your condition, your goals and how you respond to treatment. Your therapist will discuss a realistic outlook after your assessment and revisit it as you progress."},{"q":"How do I get started?","a":"Call us at (248) 415-2500 or use our contact page. Our team can answer your questions and explain the next steps for scheduling an evaluation."}],
