    extraSections: [{"heading": "Areas speech and language therapy may address", "body": ["Depending on the evaluation, therapy may address word finding and naming, understanding spoken and written language, speech clarity, social communication such as turn taking and staying on topic, and cognitive-communication skills such as attention, memory, problem solving and organizing information.", "In rehabilitation settings, speech-language pathologists also commonly evaluate swallowing concerns. If you have a specific need, call (248) 415-2500 and ask how it would be addressed."]}],
    faqs: [{"q": "Is speech therapy the same as speech and language pathology?", "a": "Usually, yes. Speech and language pathology is the formal name of the discipline, and speech therapy is the everyday term for it."}, {"q": "What are cognitive-communication problems?", "a": "They are communication difficulties that come from changes in thinking skills, such as attention, memory, organization or reasoning, rather than from the speech muscles themselves. They are common after brain injury."}, {"q": "Can therapy help years after an injury?", "a": "Many people continue to work on communication long after an injury. An evaluation can help show whether therapy is likely to be useful now and which goals are realistic."}, {"q": "Can family members be involved?", "a": "Yes. Family members often help by learning communication strategies and practicing them at home, and the team can show them how."}, {"q": "How soon can I be seen?", "a": "The practice is accepting new patients and notes that, based on your availability, it should be able to see you within seven days. Call (248) 415-2500 to start."}],
